              "textField": "Histamine is released from cells of the immune system in response to allergens, including viral and bacterial particles, resulting in vasodilation and increased permeability of the blood vessels. The *antihistamines* like diphenhydramine and loratadine bind to the Histamine 1 receptors, preventing some of the histamine binding. Conversely, the *decongestants* bind to the alpha 1 receptor on the same blood vessels, constricting them. Decongestants include *oxymetazoline*, which is only available as a nasal spray. Precautions include not to use for longer than 3 days, as it can cause *rebound congestion*. Another common decongestant includes *phenylephrine*, which causes *no therapeutic effect in most clinical trials*. *pseudoephedrine* is a very effective decongestant but can cause *CNS stimulation*"
            },
